{\b\fs32 IMPORTANT: WPods needs Windows Test Mode}\par
Please read this before you install.\par
WPods installs its own kernel drivers. They are test-signed by the WPods project, not signed by Microsoft.\par
To load them, Setup turns on Windows test signing (Test Mode). A restart is needed.\par
Test Mode requires Secure Boot to be off. If it is on, Setup stops and explains how to turn it off: restart into UEFI/BIOS, set Secure Boot to Disabled, save, then run Setup again. Have your BitLocker recovery key ready before changing Secure Boot.\par
{\b Security:} while Test Mode is on, Windows loads any test-signed kernel driver, not only WPods' drivers. This lowers a Windows security protection for as long as Test Mode stays on.\par
{\b BitLocker:} Setup suspends BitLocker for one restart, so turning on test signing does not trigger a recovery prompt.\par
{\b The Test Mode watermark:} with Test Mode on, Windows shows a "Test Mode" watermark in the corner of your desktop. WPods leaves it alone. If it bothers you, you can hide it with no-test-mode-wm (https://github.com/FPNDev/no-test-mode-wm). It is a separate, unofficial tool that hooks into Windows Explorer, so it may stop working after Windows updates. Read its README before you use it.\par
{\b This is reversible.} Uninstalling WPods can turn Test Mode off for you, or you can turn it off yourself: run bcdedit /set testsigning off in an administrator command prompt, then restart. Once Test Mode is off, you can turn Secure Boot back on.\par
You do this at your own risk. WPods comes with no warranty, as described in the no-warranty and liability terms below.\par
WPods is a free, open-source Windows app that connects to Apple AirPods and lets you use their features on a Windows PC. It includes its own Bluetooth audio drivers.\par
By installing or using WPods you agree to these terms. If you do not agree, do not install or use WPods.\par
