{\b\fs24 7. Drivers and test mode}\par
WPods installs its own kernel-mode drivers on your PC. They are test-signed by the WPods project and are not signed by Microsoft. So that Windows can load them, Setup turns on Windows test signing (test mode), which needs a restart and needs Secure Boot to be off; if Secure Boot is on, Setup stops and explains how to turn it off, and installation cannot continue until it is off. While test mode is on, Windows will load any test-signed kernel driver, not only WPods' own, which lowers a Windows security protection for as long as test mode stays on. Windows shows a "Test Mode" watermark on the desktop while test mode is on; WPods does not add or remove it, but a separate open-source tool to hide it is available at https://github.com/FPNDev/no-test-mode-wm (use it at your own risk). This is reversible: uninstalling WPods can turn test mode back off, and you can turn test signing off yourself from an administrator command prompt with bcdedit /set testsigning off, which restores the default protection. You install and use the drivers, and run your PC in test mode, at your own risk.\par
